= 1
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take positive $\theta$ toward positive $x$ from the downward vertical, as in the source diagram. The mass has position and velocity
$$
\mathbf r=(a_x+l\sin\theta,\ a_y-l\cos\theta,0),\qquad
\dot{\mathbf r}=(\dot a_x+l\dot\theta\cos\theta,\ \dot a_y+l\dot\theta\sin\theta,0).
$$
Thus $T=m|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2/2$ and $V=mg(a_y-l\cos\theta)$. Expanding the squared speed gives
$$
\boxed{L=\frac m2\left[\dot a_x^2+\dot a_y^2+l^2\dot\theta^2
+2l\dot\theta(\dot a_x\cos\theta+\dot a_y\sin\theta)\right]
-mg(a_y-l\cos\theta).}
$$
The prescribed support coordinates are functions of time, rather than additional dynamical variables. Terms depending only on time do not affect the <Euler-Lagrange equation> for $\theta$, but have been retained here to obtain the inertial-frame kinetic and <potential energies> directly.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The derivatives needed in the <Euler-Lagrange equation> are
$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot\theta}
=ml^2\dot\theta+ml(\dot a_x\cos\theta+\dot a_y\sin\theta),
$$
$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\theta}
=ml\dot\theta(-\dot a_x\sin\theta+\dot a_y\cos\theta)-mgl\sin\theta.
$$
The terms proportional to $\dot a_i\dot\theta$ cancel after differentiation, leaving
$$
\boxed{l\ddot\theta+\ddot a_x\cos\theta+(g+\ddot a_y)\sin\theta=0.}
$$
With $\mathbf l=l(\sin\theta,-\cos\theta,0)$ and $\mathbf g=-g\hat{\mathbf y}$,
$$
[\mathbf l\times(\mathbf g-\ddot{\mathbf a})]_z
=-l\left[\ddot a_x\cos\theta+(g+\ddot a_y)\sin\theta\right]
=l^2\ddot\theta.
$$
Hence the vector equation is **$l^2\ddot\theta\hat{\mathbf z}=\mathbf l\times(\mathbf g-\ddot{\mathbf a})$**. In the translating support frame, $-m\ddot{\mathbf a}$ is the <inertial force>. The rod's <tension> passes through the hinge and contributes no <torque> about it. The same <torque> equation therefore has an immediate mechanical interpretation.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For horizontal forcing the exact angular equation is
$$
\ddot\theta=-\frac gl\sin\theta+\frac{a\omega^2}{l}\cos\theta\cos\omega t.
$$
Write $\theta=\Theta+\xi$ with $\Theta=\theta_1$ slowly varying and $\xi=-(a/l)\cos\Theta\cos\omega t$. To leading order in the slow-to-fast frequency ratio, $\ddot\xi=(a\omega^2/l)\cos\Theta\cos\omega t$, which balances the rapid forcing. Expand the right-hand side to first order in $\xi$:
$$
\ddot\Theta+\ddot\xi
=-\frac gl(\sin\Theta+\xi\cos\Theta)
+\frac{a\omega^2}{l}(\cos\Theta-\xi\sin\Theta)\cos\omega t.
$$
During one fast period treat $\Theta$ as fixed. The averages $\langle\xi\rangle$ and $\langle\cos\omega t\rangle$ vanish, whereas
$$
\langle\xi\cos\omega t\rangle=-\frac{a}{2l}\cos\Theta.
$$
The leading averaged equation is therefore
$$
\boxed{\ddot\Theta=-\frac gl\sin\Theta+
\frac{a^2\omega^2}{2l^2}\sin\Theta\cos\Theta.}
$$
The effective force comes from correlation between the rapid displacement and the rapidly varying driving <torque>; simply averaging the driving cosine to zero misses it. This keeps the leading nonzero fast-motion contribution, proportional to $a^2\omega^2$, while neglecting terms suppressed by additional powers of $a/l$ or of the slow rate divided by $\omega$. The gravitational expansion at order $g(a/l)^2$ is smaller in the stated high-frequency regime. The resulting slow frequencies must also remain small compared with $\omega$ for averaging to be self-consistent.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For a fixed-pivot pendulum the angular inertia is $ml^2$, so $ml^2\ddot\Theta=-dV_{\rm eff}/d\Theta$. Integrating the averaged force gives, up to an arbitrary additive constant,
$$
\boxed{V_{\rm eff}(\Theta)=-mgl\cos\Theta+
\frac{ma^2\omega^2}{4}\cos^2\Theta.}
$$
Linearizing about $\Theta=0$ gives
$$
\ddot\Theta+\left(\frac gl-\frac{a^2\omega^2}{2l^2}\right)\Theta=0,
\qquad V_{\rm eff}''(0)=mgl-\frac{ma^2\omega^2}{2}.
$$
Hence the downward state becomes linearly unstable for
$$
\boxed{a>a_c=\frac{\sqrt{2gl}}{\omega}.}
$$
At $a=a_c$ its quadratic restoring term vanishes: the leading quartic coefficient is positive, so it is marginal at linear order, rather than already a local maximum. Above threshold the two stable tilted equilibria satisfy $\cos\Theta=2gl/(a^2\omega^2)$. Horizontal vibration therefore destabilizes the downward orientation and produces tilted minima; it does not stabilize the upward orientation in this model. Because $a_c/l=\sqrt2\sqrt{g/l}/\omega\ll1$, the threshold is compatible with the assumed small support excursion.

\Image[paper-1-horizontal-pendulum.png]
{title=Original averaged pendulum potentials below, at and above the horizontal-vibration instability threshold}

= 2
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Every element of the uniformly distributed mass has speed squared $\dot r^2+r^2\dot\phi^2$, so the total <kinetic energy> is $m(\dot r^2+r^2\dot\phi^2)/2$. The extension of the ring's full circumference is $2\pi(r-r_0)$, and the stated <spring constant> therefore gives
$$
V=\frac k2[2\pi(r-r_0)]^2=2\pi^2k(r-r_0)^2.
$$
The magnetic coupling for each charge element is $dq\,\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf v$. With the <symmetric gauge> $\mathbf A=(Br/2)\hat{\boldsymbol\phi}$ and tangential speed $r\dot\phi$, integration over the ring gives $qBr^2\dot\phi/2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{L=\frac m2\dot r^2+\frac{mr^2}{2}\dot\phi^2
-2\pi^2k(r-r_0)^2+\frac{qBr^2}{2}\dot\phi.}
$$
This idealization neglects charge self-energy, radiation and deformation away from a circular ring. The elastic constant refers to extension of the whole circumference; replacing it by a radial <spring constant> without the factor $4\pi^2$ changes the problem.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <canonical momenta> are
$$
p_r=m\dot r,\qquad p_\phi=mr^2\dot\phi+\frac{qBr^2}{2}.
$$
The <Legendre transform> cancels the velocity-linear magnetic term, giving the energy expressed in velocities,
$$
\boxed{H=\frac m2\dot r^2+\frac{mr^2}{2}\dot\phi^2+2\pi^2k(r-r_0)^2.}
$$
But <Hamilton's equations> differentiate with respect to canonical coordinates and momenta, not velocities. In its proper <canonical variables> the same <Hamiltonian> is
$$
\boxed{H(r,\phi,p_r,p_\phi)
=\frac{p_r^2}{2m}+
\frac{(p_\phi-qBr^2/2)^2}{2mr^2}
+2\pi^2k(r-r_0)^2.}
$$
It explicitly contains $B$ because canonical <angular momentum> differs from mechanical <angular momentum>. This reconciles magnetic forces with the absence of magnetic work: the <magnetic field> can exchange radial and tangential motion without changing the total <mechanical energy>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Time-translation symmetry conserves the energy $H$, and axial rotational symmetry makes $\phi$ a <cyclic coordinate>, conserving
$$
\boxed{J=p_\phi=mr^2\dot\phi+\frac{qBr^2}{2}.}
$$
The radial <Euler-Lagrange equation> before elimination is
$$
m\ddot r=mr\dot\phi^2+qBr\dot\phi-4\pi^2k(r-r_0).
$$
Insert $\dot\phi=J/(mr^2)-qB/(2m)$. The cross terms linear in $JqB$ cancel, leaving
$$
\boxed{m\ddot r=\frac{J^2}{mr^3}-\frac{q^2B^2}{4m}r-4\pi^2k(r-r_0).}
$$
Equivalently use the reduced radial potential
$$
U_J(r)=\frac{J^2}{2mr^2}+\frac{q^2B^2r^2}{8m}
+2\pi^2k(r-r_0)^2-\frac{JqB}{2m}.
$$
The last term is constant at fixed $J$ and has no radial derivative, but is part of the full conserved energy. Conservation of $J$ is stated in the rotationally <symmetric gauge>; the conserved mechanical combination can be written in terms of <magnetic flux> in a gauge-independent way.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The initial <angular velocity> cancels the flux term in the canonical <angular momentum>:
$$
J=mr_0^2\left(-\frac{qB}{2m}\right)+\frac{qBr_0^2}{2}=0.
$$
Define $\omega_c=qB/m$ and $\omega_s^2=4\pi^2k/m$. The radial equation becomes $\ddot r+\Omega^2r=\omega_s^2r_0$, so
$$
\boxed{\Omega^2=\omega_s^2+\frac{\omega_c^2}{4},\qquad
r_e=\frac{\omega_s^2}{\omega_s^2+\omega_c^2/4}r_0,\qquad
r(t)=r_e+(r_0-r_e)\cos\Omega t.}
$$
These identify the paper's $\omega$ with $\Omega$. For nonzero radius, $J=0$ also fixes
$$
\boxed{\phi(t)=-\frac{\omega_c}{2}t,\qquad\phi(0)=0.}
$$
A radius is nonnegative. The formal sinusoidal solution remains strictly positive for a full cycle only if $2r_e>r_0$, or $\omega_s^2>\omega_c^2/4$. At equality it touches zero; at stronger magnetic coupling it first reaches zero and its negative continuation is not a physical ring radius. No collision or re-expansion rule at collapse is supplied, so the circular-ring solution is then valid only up to the first zero. This domain qualification leaves the requested differential-equation solution intact.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Take the stated negligible-natural-radius limit as $r_0=0$, with both initial velocities zero at $r=r_1$. Then $J=qBr_1^2/2$. Write $\omega_c=qB/m$ and $\Omega^2=\omega_s^2+\omega_c^2/4$, where $\omega_s^2=4\pi^2k/m$. At two radial turning points $x=r^2$, equality of the effective radial energies gives
$$
x_{\max}x_{\min}=\frac{J^2}{m^2\Omega^2}.
$$
Using $x_{\max}=r_1^2$ gives, for $qB\ne0$ and $k>0$,
$$
\boxed{r_{\min}=r_1\frac{|\omega_c|}{2\Omega}
=\frac{|qB|\,r_1}{\sqrt{q^2B^2+16\pi^2km}}.}
$$
The minimum is a regular radial turning point, so
$$
\boxed{\dot r_{\min}=0,\qquad
\dot\phi_{\min}=\frac J{mr_{\min}^2}-\frac{\omega_c}{2}
=\frac{2\omega_s^2}{\omega_c}=\frac{8\pi^2k}{qB}.}
$$
The <angular velocity> has the sign of $qB$. On the initial inward motion, conservation of canonical <angular momentum> produces the rotation needed to prevent collapse to zero radius.

The strictly zero-field case is singular for this angular formula: $J=0$ and the ring instead collapses to $r=0$ with inward radial speed $\omega_s r_1$ in the ideal radial equation, where an angular coordinate ceases to describe a ring. The limit $qB\to0$ through nonzero fields gives a tiny positive turning radius with large <angular velocity>, and must not be substituted into the formula at exactly zero field. A small nonzero $r_0$ also matters if the predicted minimum becomes comparable with it.

= 3
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For canonical coordinates $(q_i,p_i)$, the <Poisson bracket> is
$$
\boxed{\{F,G\}=\sum_i\left(
\frac{\partial F}{\partial q_i}\frac{\partial G}{\partial p_i}
-\frac{\partial F}{\partial p_i}\frac{\partial G}{\partial q_i}\right).}
$$
It is bilinear and antisymmetric, obeys the <Leibniz rule> in either argument, and satisfies the <Jacobi identity>. These properties encode the symplectic structure of <phase space>. The two printed inline applications are treated separately below.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An invertible change $(q,p)\mapsto(Q,P)$ is canonical when it preserves the <Poisson bracket>. Its fundamental brackets must be
$$
\boxed{\{Q_i,Q_j\}=0,\qquad\{P_i,P_j\}=0,\qquad
\{Q_i,P_j\}=\delta_{ij}.}
$$
The <chain rule> then makes the bracket of any two <observables> identical in the old and new coordinates. Consequently <Hamilton's equations> retain their canonical form. A time-dependent <canonical transformation> also requires the corresponding extra term in the transformed <Hamiltonian>; bracket preservation by itself concerns each fixed time.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Along <Hamiltonian> motion,
$$
\frac{dO}{dt}=\frac{\partial O}{\partial t}
+\sum_i\left(\frac{\partial O}{\partial q_i}\dot q_i
+\frac{\partial O}{\partial p_i}\dot p_i\right).
$$
Substituting $\dot q_i=\partial H/\partial p_i$ and $\dot p_i=-\partial H/\partial q_i$ gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{dO}{dt}=\frac{\partial O}{\partial t}+\{O,H\}.}
$$
For the printed $O(q,p)$ there is no explicit time term. In particular, an autonomous <observable> is conserved when its <Poisson bracket> with $H$ vanishes. Taking $O=q_i$ or $O=p_i$ recovers <Hamilton's equations>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $H=p\cosh(2q)$,
$$
\boxed{\dot q=\cosh(2q),\qquad \dot p=-2p\sinh(2q).}
$$
The derivatives of $O=e^{qp}$ are $O_q=pe^{qp}$ and $O_p=qe^{qp}$. Its <Poisson bracket> with $H$ is therefore
$$
\boxed{\dot O=\{O,H\}=p e^{qp}\left[\cosh(2q)-2q\sinh(2q)\right].}
$$
Direct differentiation, $\dot O=e^{qp}(p\dot q+q\dot p)$, gives the same expression and checks the bracket sign.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In one canonical degree of freedom it is enough to check $\{Q,P\}=1$ for a locally invertible transformation. Here
$$
\{Q,P\}=ff'(\cosh^2q-\sinh^2q)=ff',
$$
so $ff'=1$ and $f(p)^2=2p+C$. To obtain exactly the requested <Hamiltonian>, choose $C=0$ and the positive branch for $p>0$:
$$
\boxed{f(p)=\sqrt{2p},\qquad
Q=\sqrt{2p}\sinh q,\quad P=\sqrt{2p}\cosh q.}
$$
The identities $\cosh^2q-\sinh^2q=1$ and $\cosh^2q+\sinh^2q=\cosh2q$ give
$$
\boxed{\frac{Q^2+P^2}{2}=p\cosh2q=H.}
$$
This is a local canonical chart, with inverse
$$
p=\frac{P^2-Q^2}{2},\qquad q=\operatorname{artanh}(Q/P),\qquad P>|Q|.
$$
It is not a global identification of the real $(q,p>0)$ half-plane with the entire oscillator plane. The negative branch for $f$ gives the analogous lower wedge. This domain matters if an oscillator trajectory is continued beyond the boundary of the chart.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The perturbed <Hamiltonian> is $H'=(Q^2+P^2)/2-g(t)Q$. <Hamilton's equations> give $\dot Q=P$, $\dot P=-Q+g(t)$, hence
$$
\boxed{\ddot Q+Q=g(t).}
$$
In the paper's time units the natural frequency is one. The <retarded Green function> is $G_R(t)=\Theta(t)\sin t$: it vanishes before the source, is continuous at zero and has a unit derivative jump there. With Fourier convention $e^{-i\omega t}$,
$$
\boxed{G_R(\omega)=\frac1{1-(\omega+i0^+)^2}.}
$$
Both real-frequency poles are displaced below the real axis. <Causality> therefore selects the convolution
$$
Q_R(t)=\int_{-\infty}^t\sin(t-t')\frac{\omega_0}{2}e^{-\omega_0|t'|}\,dt'.
$$
For $t<0$ the exponential rises as $e^{\omega_0t'}$ throughout the integral. For $t\ge0$, split the integral at $t'=0$. Evaluation yields
$$
\boxed{Q_R(t)=\frac{\omega_0}{2(1+\omega_0^2)}
\begin{cases}
e^{\omega_0 t},&t<0,\\
e^{-\omega_0t}+2\omega_0\sin t,&t\ge0.
\end{cases}}
$$
The response is nonzero before $t=0$ because the prescribed force is already nonzero then; this does not violate <causality>. Both $Q$ and $\dot Q$ are continuous at zero. The force eventually decays, leaving the free oscillation $\omega_0^2\sin t/(1+\omega_0^2)$. A general solution adds $A\cos t+B\sin t$, but the retarded condition of no incoming free oscillation as $t\to-\infty$ fixes $A=B=0$.

The oscillator equation is well-defined on the full $(Q,P)$ plane. Interpreted through the positive-$p$ chart of part (c), it describes the original real variables only while $P>|Q|$, with $P=\dot Q$. The requested retarded oscillator solution is a continuation in the new variables; it is not a claim that this canonical chart remains valid at all times for every $\omega_0$.

\Image[paper-1-retarded-oscillator.png]
{title=Original retarded response to a two-sided exponential force; the pre-zero response follows earlier nonzero forcing}

= 4
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

An element with unperturbed coordinate length $dx$ has mass $\rho\,dx$ and transverse speed squared $\dot\psi_y^2+\dot\psi_z^2$. Its actual length is
$$
ds=\sqrt{1+(\psi_y')^2+(\psi_z')^2}\,dx
\simeq\left[1+\frac12((\psi_y')^2+(\psi_z')^2)\right]dx.
$$
At fixed <tension> the extra <elastic energy> to this order is $F(ds-dx)$. Subtracting it from the <kinetic energy> gives
$$
\boxed{L=\int dx\left[\frac\rho2(\dot\psi_y^2+\dot\psi_z^2)
-\frac F2((\psi_y')^2+(\psi_z')^2)\right].}
$$
Here prime means $\partial_x$ and dot means $\partial_t$. The approximation retains quadratic transverse energy and neglects higher-order slope effects and longitudinal motion. The resulting field equations are $\rho\ddot\psi_i=F\psi_i''$ for $i=y,z$, with <wave speed> $\sqrt{F/\rho}$ when $F>0$.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Adding a constant to $\psi_y$ changes neither its time nor space derivatives, so the <action> is invariant. The associated Noether density and flux are
$$
j^0_y=\rho\dot\psi_y,\qquad j^x_y=-F\psi_y'.
$$
The <wave equation> gives $\partial_tj^0_y+\partial_xj^x_y=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P_y=\int\rho\dot\psi_y\,dx,\qquad
\frac{dP_y}{dt}=F[\psi_y']_{\rm left}^{\rm right}.}
$$
This is total physical transverse momentum. It is conserved for a whole isolated infinite string with vanishing boundary flux, periodic <boundary conditions>, or endpoints exerting no net transverse force. For a finite subsystem momentum can flow through its ends, and fixed supports can exchange momentum with the string. Thus the local <conservation law> always holds in this model, while constancy of the integrated charge requires the stated <boundary condition>.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

An infinitesimal rotation about $x$ gives $\delta\psi_y=-\epsilon\psi_z$ and $\delta\psi_z=\epsilon\psi_y$. Both the squared transverse velocity and squared transverse slope are rotational invariants. <Noether's theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\ell_x=\rho(\psi_y\dot\psi_z-\psi_z\dot\psi_y),\qquad
\mathcal J_x=-F(\psi_y\psi_z'-\psi_z\psi_y').}
$$
Differentiating and using the two <wave equations> gives
$$
\partial_t\ell_x=F(\psi_y\psi_z''-\psi_z\psi_y'')
=-\partial_x\mathcal J_x.
$$
Consequently **$L_x=\int\ell_x\,dx$ is the <angular momentum> about the equilibrium string axis**, conserved when the net endpoint <torque> flux vanishes. For a finite segment, $dL_x/dt=-[\mathcal J_x]_{\rm left}^{\rm right}$. It is not momentum associated with translating the coordinate $x$; its symmetry rotates the two physical transverse displacement components into one another.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For one transverse displacement $\psi$ the density is
$$
\mathcal L=\frac\rho2\psi_t^2-\frac F2\psi_x^2-\frac B2\psi_{xx}^2.
$$
Its variation is $\delta S=\int dt\,dx[\rho\psi_t\delta\psi_t-F\psi_x\delta\psi_x-B\psi_{xx}\delta\psi_{xx}]$. Integrating once in time and twice in space gives the bulk coefficient
$$
-\rho\psi_{tt}+F\psi_{xx}-B\psi_{xxxx}=0.
$$
The spatial boundary contribution is $[(-F\psi_x+B\psi_{xxx})\delta\psi-B\psi_{xx}\delta\psi_x]$ integrated over time. Thus
$$
\boxed{\rho\psi_{tt}=F\psi_{xx}-B\psi_{xxxx}.}
$$
Insert $\psi\propto e^{i(kx-\omega t)}$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{\omega^2=\frac{Fk^2+Bk^4}{\rho}=\frac{k^2}{\rho}(F+Bk^2).}
$$
For compression $F<0$, sufficiently long waves have $\omega^2<0$ and grow exponentially. Positive bending rigidity stabilizes short wavelengths; without it the compressed small-slope model would have no short-wavelength regularization.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

With endpoints held at zero displacement but free to rotate, variation permits arbitrary endpoint $\delta\psi_x$ and gives the natural zero-moment condition $\psi_{xx}=0$ there. These are simply supported endpoints. The <normal modes> are
$$
\psi_n(x,t)=A_n(t)\sin(n\pi x/l),\qquad k_n=n\pi/l.
$$
The first mode becomes unstable when $F+B(\pi/l)^2<0$, so
$$
\boxed{l>\pi\sqrt{\frac{B}{-F}}\quad(F<0).}
$$
Equivalently the compressive load exceeds $|F|_{\rm crit}=\pi^2B/l^2$. At equality the linear mode is neutral and nonlinear terms are needed to determine its post-buckling amplitude. The quadratic energy of $A\sin(\pi x/l)$ is $A^2l[F(\pi/l)^2+B(\pi/l)^4]/4$, giving the same sign test.

The endpoint displacement condition alone does not impose clamping. The quoted threshold uses the natural free-slope <boundary condition> of the stated variational problem. If rotations were also fixed, the admissible modes and threshold would change; one must not import a clamped-end formula into this result.

\Image[paper-1-compressed-rod.png]
{title=Original bending-versus-compression dispersion and the first simply supported buckling mode}

= 5
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use $\hbar=c=1$, signature $(+---)$, and define $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+ieA_\mu$. Under a local phase transformation,
$$
\phi'=e^{-ie\alpha(x)}\phi,\qquad
A'_\mu=A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha,
$$
the derivative of the phase cancels the change of the potential, giving $D'_\mu\phi'=e^{-ie\alpha}D_\mu\phi$. Therefore a gauge-invariant scalar-electromagnetic <Lagrangian> density is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L=(D_\mu\phi)^*D^\mu\phi-m^2\phi^*\phi
-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu},\qquad
F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu.}
$$
The mass term is invariant, the covariant-derivative product has canceling phases, and $F'_{\mu\nu}=F_{\mu\nu}$ because mixed <partial derivatives> commute. A potential depending only on $\phi^*\phi$ may be added without changing the symmetry. The Maxwell term makes the electromagnetic field dynamical; it can be omitted when $A_\mu$ is a prescribed external background.

Expanding the covariant-derivative term gives the interaction linear in $e$ plus **$e^2A_\mu A^\mu|\phi|^2$**. This quadratic term is required for exact local <gauge invariance>. The later part (c) deliberately gives only the linear interaction, and must be solved as that different stated <Lagrangian> rather than silently adding the missing term.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For fields $\varphi_A$ and a continuous infinitesimal internal symmetry $\delta\varphi_A=\epsilon\Delta\varphi_A$, suppose the density changes by a <divergence>, $\delta\mathcal L=\epsilon\partial_\mu K^\mu$. Varying the <action> and using the <Euler-Lagrange equations> gives the <Noether current>
$$
\boxed{j^\mu=\sum_A\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\varphi_A)}\Delta\varphi_A-K^\mu,
\qquad\partial_\mu j^\mu=0.}
$$
The <conserved charge> is $Q=\int j^0\,d^3x$ when the flux through the boundary vanishes; more generally $dQ/dt=-\oint\mathbf j\cdot d\mathbf S$. If the <Lagrangian> is exactly invariant, $K^\mu=0$. Treat a complex field and its conjugate as independent variables during variation, imposing conjugacy on physical solutions. Time and space translations give energy and momentum through the corresponding spacetime version of the theorem. Continuous global symmetry gives an ordinary charge; local gauge redundancy requires additional care and is not an independent <conserved charge> for every arbitrary function.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Treat $\phi$ and $\phi^*$ as independent fields, keep $A_\mu(x)$ as the prescribed background, and use the source's interaction with no added $e^2A^2|\phi|^2$ term. The momentum derivatives are
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\phi)}
=\partial^\mu\phi^*-ieA^\mu\phi^*,\qquad
\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\phi^*)}
=\partial^\mu\phi+ieA^\mu\phi.
$$
Varying $\phi^*$ and $\phi$ gives respectively
$$
\boxed{(\Box+m^2)\phi+ie(\partial_\mu A^\mu)\phi
+2ieA^\mu\partial_\mu\phi=0,}
$$
$$
\boxed{(\Box+m^2)\phi^*-ie(\partial_\mu A^\mu)\phi^*
-2ieA^\mu\partial_\mu\phi^*=0.}
$$
No <Lorenz gauge condition> has been assumed. The background <divergence> term must be retained unless such a condition is separately imposed.

The <canonical momenta> conjugate to the two scalar fields are
$$
\pi=\dot\phi^*-ieA_0\phi^*,\qquad
\pi^*=\dot\phi+ieA_0\phi,
$$
so $\dot\phi=\pi^*-ieA_0\phi$ and $\dot\phi^*=\pi+ieA_0\phi^*$. Define the physical three-vector potential by $A_\mu=(A_0,-\mathbf A)$. The <Legendre transform> in the proper <canonical variables> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal H(\phi,\phi^*,\pi,\pi^*;x,t)
={}&(\pi+ieA_0\phi^*)(\pi^*-ieA_0\phi)
+\boldsymbol\nabla\phi^*\cdot\boldsymbol\nabla\phi+m^2|\phi|^2\\
&-ie\mathbf A\cdot(\phi\boldsymbol\nabla\phi^*-\phi^*\boldsymbol\nabla\phi).
\end{aligned}}
$$
Equivalently the first product is $\pi\pi^*+ieA_0(\phi^*\pi^*-\phi\pi)+e^2A_0^2|\phi|^2$. The last quadratic term arises from replacing velocities by <canonical momenta> even though the original <Lagrangian> has no quadratic interaction. It is not permission to modify the specified <Lagrangian>. An explicitly time-dependent background generally makes the scalar-field energy time dependent.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $\Delta\phi=-i\phi$ and $\Delta\phi^*=i\phi^*$, the interaction is invariant under the constant phase because every product has net zero phase. Applying the Noether formula to the two independent fields gives
$$
\boxed{J^\mu=i(\phi^*\partial^\mu\phi-\phi\partial^\mu\phi^*)
-2eA^\mu|\phi|^2.}
$$
The sign corresponds to the printed transformation $\phi\to e^{-i\epsilon}\phi$. Reversing the phase parameter would reverse the entire current, with no change in conservation.

The derivative products in the <divergence> of the first term cancel pairwise:
$$
\partial_\mu J^\mu=i(\phi^*\Box\phi-\phi\Box\phi^*)
-2e\left[(\partial_\mu A^\mu)|\phi|^2+A^\mu\partial_\mu|\phi|^2\right].
$$
The two field equations from part (c) imply
$$
\phi^*\Box\phi-\phi\Box\phi^*
=-2ie\left[(\partial_\mu A^\mu)|\phi|^2
+A^\mu\partial_\mu|\phi|^2\right].
$$
Multiplication by $i$ exactly cancels the remaining background terms, proving **$\partial_\mu J^\mu=0$** without requiring $A_\mu$ to be constant or <divergence> free. The global phase symmetry survives even though this truncated interaction is not exactly invariant under arbitrary local <gauge transformations>.

= 6
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Mean-field theory> replaces fluctuating interactions with the average field produced by the other degrees of freedom. For an interacting <spin> system, write $\mathbf S_i=\mathbf S+\delta\mathbf S_i$ and neglect connected products $\delta\mathbf S_i\cdot\delta\mathbf S_j$. This reduces the many-body <partition function> to independent single-site factors coupled through a <self-consistency equation> for $\mathbf S$.

The approximation is most reliable when many effectively independent neighbors contribute, for sufficiently long-range interactions, or above the <upper critical dimension> away from the fluctuation-dominated critical region. It usually becomes least reliable near a continuous transition in low dimensions, where correlations extend over long distances. It can estimate ordering scales and reveal candidate broken symmetries while giving incorrect <critical exponents> or even a spurious transition. The two-dimensional isotropic model in the later parts is an especially important example of this limitation.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let the coordination number be $z_c=4$, and choose the spin-space $z$ axis along $\mathbf S=S\hat{\mathbf z}$. The mean-field decoupling of a bond is
$$
\mathbf S_i\cdot\mathbf S_j\simeq
\mathbf S_i\cdot\mathbf S+\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf S_j-S^2.
$$
The original directed neighbor sum counts each bond twice, so its factor $1/2$ cancels that double counting. The resulting energy is
$$
\boxed{E_{\rm MF}=-z_cJS\sum_i S_{iz}+\frac{Nz_cJ}{2}S^2.}
$$
The constant prevents double counting of the mean interaction energy and is necessary if <free energy> is minimized with respect to $S$. With the source's unnormalized solid-angle measure, the single-site integral is
$$
Z_1(x)=2\pi\int_{-1}^{1}e^{x u}\,du
=4\pi\frac{\sinh x}{x},\qquad x=\beta z_cJS.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{Z_{\rm MF}(S)=e^{-\beta Nz_cJS^2/2}
\left(4\pi\frac{\sinh(\beta z_cJS)}{\beta z_cJS}\right)^N,
\qquad z_c=4.}
$$
At $S=0$ take $\sinh x/x\to1$. A normalized solid-angle measure would remove the factor $(4\pi)^N$ without changing the <self-consistency equation>. These are three-component classical <spins> on a two-dimensional positional lattice, not <spins> constrained to a plane.

The PDF's hint is a correct angular-integration identity for a term with coefficient $\beta J/2$. That coefficient is not the full single-site molecular field after summing all four neighbors and decoupling both entries of each bond. The net coefficient is $\beta z_cJ$, as follows from the explicitly stated <Hamiltonian>; inserting the hint's coefficient as the total field would lose the coordination and double-counting factors.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In <mean-field theory> all sites have the same normalized single-site distribution $e^{x\cos\theta}/Z_1(x)$. Consequently
$$
\langle S_{kz}\rangle=\frac{d\log Z_1}{dx}
=\coth x-\frac1x\equiv\mathcal L(x),
$$
where $\mathcal L$ is the <Langevin function>. Requiring the average used to construct the field to equal the average in that field gives
$$
\boxed{S=\coth(\beta z_cJS)-\frac1{\beta z_cJS}.}
$$
Set
$$
\boxed{x=\beta z_cJS=\frac{4JS}{k_BT},\qquad
\tau=\frac1{\beta z_cJ}=\frac{k_BT}{4J}.}
$$
Since $S=\tau x$, the <self-consistency equation> is exactly **$\tau x=\coth x-1/x$**. It also follows by stationarity of $F_{\rm MF}/N=z_cJS^2/2-k_BT\log Z_1(\beta z_cJS)$. This independent route checks the constant term and all neighbor-counting factors in the <partition function>.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Near zero the <Langevin function> has expansion
$$
\mathcal L(x)=\frac x3-\frac{x^3}{45}+O(x^5).
$$
For positive $x$, $\mathcal L(x)$ rises toward one and its ratio $\mathcal L(x)/x$ decreases from $1/3$ to zero. Therefore a line $\tau x$ has only the zero intersection for $\tau\ge1/3$, and also a nonzero positive intersection for $0<\tau<1/3$. In a fixed signed component there is a corresponding negative solution; in the isotropic vector problem the nonzero solutions have all spin-space orientations.

The mean-field transition temperature is
$$
\boxed{k_BT_c^{\rm MF}=\frac{z_cJ}{3}=\frac{4J}{3}.}
$$
At the <critical temperature> the line is tangent at the origin. Just below it, $S\simeq\sqrt{(5/3)(1-T/T_c^{\rm MF})}$, the usual mean-field square-root onset. The ordered minimum is stable below this temperature while $S=0$ becomes unstable.

**This is a mean-field prediction, not a genuine finite-temperature ordering transition for the ideal two-dimensional short-range isotropic model.** Long-wavelength <spin> fluctuations invalidate the predicted nonzero thermodynamic <magnetization> at every $T>0$. The relevant obstruction is the <Mermin-Wagner theorem> for continuous <spin> symmetry; anisotropy, long-range interactions or a finite sample change the assumptions. Its original paper establishes the absence of finite-temperature magnetic ordering for one- and two-dimensional finite-range isotropic <Heisenberg models>.<Absence of Ferromagnetism or Antiferromagnetism in One- or Two-Dimensional Isotropic Heisenberg Models by N. D. Mermin and H. Wagner>[Physical Review Letters 17, 1133 (1966), abstract; physical limitation of finite-temperature mean-field order]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-langevin-mean-field.png]
{title=Original Langevin self-consistency plot and the mean-field order parameter, explicitly distinguished from true two-dimensional isotropic long-range order}

= e
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For uniform unconstrained <boundary conditions>, the positive <gradient> coefficient favors a spatially constant minimizer. Put $u=|\mathbf m|^2\ge0$. The remaining free-energy difference is $a(T-T_c)u+bu^2$. Its minimum is
$$
\boxed{\begin{cases}
\mathbf m=0,&T\ge T_c,\\
\mathbf m=m_0\hat{\mathbf n},\quad
m_0=\sqrt{\dfrac{a(T_c-T)}{2b}},\quad |\hat{\mathbf n}|=1,&T<T_c.
\end{cases}}
$$
Below $T_c$ the amplitude is fixed but its direction is arbitrary; the set of uniform minima is a two-sphere. The free-energy minimum is $f_0-a^2(T_c-T)^2/(4b)$ below $T_c$ and $f_0$ above. The <order parameter> vanishes continuously, so this is a continuous, second-order transition within <Landau theory>.

The <free energy> has internal $O(3)$ spin-rotation/reflection symmetry; a chosen nonzero vector leaves an $O(2)$ subgroup invariant. Restricting to proper rotations, this is **$SO(3)\to SO(2)$ <spontaneous symmetry breaking>**. There are two transverse orientation directions and a massive amplitude direction below the transition. Spatially varying directions cost <gradient> energy, and imposed boundaries or defects can prevent a uniform minimizer. In strictly two-dimensional equilibrium with short-range interactions, the fluctuation caveat of part (d) still applies: minimizing this polynomial functional at mean-field level does not overturn the absence of finite-temperature spontaneous <magnetization>.
